Objects

1. WHAT IS AN OBJECT?
An object is a collection of related data stored as key-value pairs.Each key (also called a property name) has a value. 
The value can be a string, number, boolean, array, another object, or even a function.

Real-life example: a person has a name, an age and a city. Instead of keeping three separate variables, we group them in one object.
  const person = {
    name: "Asha",
    age: 25,
    city: "Bengaluru"
  };

- Keys are strings (or symbols).
- Values can be any type.
- A function stored in an object is called a METHOD.


2. HOW TO CREATE AN OBJECT

a Object literal (most common)

   const car = {
     brand: "Toyota",
     year: 2022,
     isElectric: false
   };

b Using the new Object() constructor

   const phone = new Object();
   phone.model = "Pixel";
   phone.price = 50000;

3. HOW TO ACCESS OBJECT PROPERTIES
Given:

   const person = {
     name: "Asha",
     age: 25,
     "favorite color": "blue",
     address: { city: "Bengaluru", pin: 560001 }
   };

a Dot notation (most common)

   person.name          // "Asha"
   person.age           // 25

b Bracket notation

   person["name"]             // "Asha"
   person["favorite color"]   // "blue"

   const key = "age";
   person[key]                // 25  (variable used as key)

c Nested properties

   person.address.city        // "Bengaluru"
   person["address"]["pin"]   // 560001

d Accessing a property that does not exist

   person.salary              // undefined

4. ADD, UPDATE, DELETE PROPERTIES
   person.email = "asha@mail.com";   // add
   person.age = 26;                  // update
   delete person.city;               // delete
   "name" in person                  // true  (check if key exists)
   person.hasOwnProperty("name")     // true

5. LOOP THROUGH PROPERTIES
   for (const key in person) {
     console.log(key, person[key]);
   }

   Object.keys(person)     // array of keys
   Object.values(person)   // array of values
   Object.entries(person)  // array of [key, value] pairs